  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through this weekend...\n\nCurrent satellite and 500mb RAP analysis this afternoon reveals a \npotent upper low centered across northern California this afternoon. \nThis feature is inducing low thickness and southwesterly flow aloft, \nwhich is fueling the below average temperatures seen the last few \ndays. Highs this afternoon are running around 10 degrees below \nnormal for the inland areas. A very deep marine layer has also \nresulted from the influence of the nearby upper low, which spread \nwell through the coastal mountain slopes, with even isolated \ninstances of drizzle along the coast this morning. As we progress \ninto this weekend, this upper low will gradually kick east towards \nthe northern Rockies, allowing a weak upper ridge to pass through. \nThis will bring a subtle warming trend to the region, bringing \ntemperatures back to near normal for Saturday/Sunday. This passing \nridge will also serve to squish down the marine layer some, \nrestricting cloud cover towards the coasts and western valleys.\n\nMonday through midweek...\n\nFollowing the subtle weekend warmup, another weak trough is set to \nswing through Monday/Tuesday. This will knock temperature back down \na few degrees, back to cooler than seasonal norms, for Monday and \nTuesday. Beyond Tuesday, models begin to divergence in the \nresolution of the upper ridge across the south-central United \nStates. Some ensemble clusters favor a stronger ridge that can push \nfurther west, with other clusters keeping the ridge weaker and \nfurther east. This is leading to a fairly large spread in \ntemperatures come mid next week, thought the NBM mean is favoring a \nwarming trend that brings temperatures around 5 degrees normal for \nWednesday.",
